t the baby underwater? If the baby stayed submerged after birth: 1. The placenta will stop delivering oxygen within about 5–10 minutes (or sooner if it separates or the cord is clamped). 1. The baby’s diving reflex can only suppress breathing for so long. Eventually, rising carbon dioxide levels will force the baby to take a breath—but there’s only water around, no air. 1. The baby would inhale water, filling the lungs that are supposed to now hold air, leading to drowning. Even if the umbilical cord were somehow still attached perfectly underwater, the baby still couldn’t get oxygen through the cord indefinitely—the placenta detaches from the uterine wall, and the cord stops working. And human lungs are not built to extract oxygen from water like gills. We are air‑breathing mammals. So, while the image of a baby swimming like a mermaid is magical, it’s biologically impossible. The transition from fluid-filled lungs and placental oxygen to air-filled lungs and breathing air must happen within minutes of birth—otherwise, the baby cannot survive.
